  "notes": "British dance-pop vocalist with a distinctive fluttering timbre, modelled in the dance mode the entry gives at 120-130 (the 90-110 pop mode is documented but not the lane this section covers). The vocal is the encoded identity: \"a light, breathy, fast-vibrato soprano written high and thin, frequently multitracked into a choir-like pad\", and \"her toplines are narrow-range, rhythmically busy and syllable-dense, with the hook often carried by a wordless multitracked vowel\". So the melody is the densest sung line in this section at 8-14 notes a bar inside a narrow high band with almost no octave jump, and the countermelody is a sustained pad rather than an answering lick — the multitracked stack. The kit is the entry's \"four-square kick, clap on 2&4, offbeat 8th hats\", with the other half of her identity, \"stomps, hand-claps and floor toms\" on the folk-facing records, kept as tom and snap lanes. The bass is \"pumping filtered synth bass on 8ths; sub-and-kick fused\", a kick mirror at 0.94. ⚠ The research marks the key/vocal reading `[LOW-CONF]` and calls the parameters lane-derived; both marks are repeated here, and the registers, densities and velocities are INFERRED.",
